It's early morning in this residential neighborhood

258
00:13:23,260 --> 00:13:26,460
of Pendleton County, Kentucky.

259
00:13:26,560 --> 00:13:30,780
But not everything here is quite as it seems.

260
00:13:30,880 --> 00:13:32,780
Because while your nose won't detect it,

261
00:13:32,880 --> 00:13:36,880
what turns their lights on around here is stink.

262
00:13:36,980 --> 00:13:41,680
Specifically, stink from the residents' own trash.

263
00:13:41,681 --> 00:13:44,720
Picked up from the curb once a week,

264
00:13:44,820 --> 00:13:48,680
and offloaded at the county landfill 15 miles away,

265
00:13:48,780 --> 00:13:52,220
where the gas from the waste is converted to electricity

266
00:13:52,320 --> 00:13:53,720
that powers neighboring homes.

267
00:14:00,240 --> 00:14:04,240
Rumpke Consolidated Companies owns and operates this landfill,

268
00:14:04,340 --> 00:14:09,340
as well as eight others across Ohio, Kentucky, and Indiana.

269
00:14:09,341 --> 00:14:11,320
At this Rumpke landfill alone,

270
00:14:11,560 --> 00:14:15,460
they take in about 1,500 tons of solid waste every day.

271
00:14:15,840 --> 00:14:17,960
We're standing at the working face of the landfill

272
00:14:17,961 --> 00:14:19,200
where trash comes daily.

273
00:14:19,420 --> 00:14:21,480
We're dumping trash that's probably a week old.

274
00:14:22,180 --> 00:14:24,180
It smells when you put it in your garbage can,

275
00:14:24,300 --> 00:14:25,780
and it smells when it gets here at the landfill.

276
00:14:26,740 --> 00:14:29,660
The noticeable stink of freshly dumped garbage

277
00:14:29,661 --> 00:14:33,020
comes from trace amounts of sulfur compounds and ammonia,

278
00:14:33,640 --> 00:14:36,620
the same odorous gases released from other decomposing matter,

279
00:14:37,120 --> 00:14:37,900
like cow manure.

280
00:14:37,901 --> 00:14:50,640
Our challenge as a landfill owner and operator is to take a waste product that smells to mitigate that odor and mitigate the impact to the surrounding community.

281
00:14:51,580 --> 00:14:58,340
For starters, specially designed landfill compactors flatten the offloaded waste to half its original volume,

282
00:15:00,460 --> 00:15:05,460
which bulldozers will cover with six inches of soil at the end of each day to bury the stench.

283
00:15:05,461 --> 00:15:12,160
In addition, Rumpke uses odor misters that spray chemical fragrances over the working

284
00:15:12,161 --> 00:15:12,861
landfill.

285
00:15:13,360 --> 00:15:15,840
The mist you see is an odor suppression chemical.

286
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:20,460
It mixes with the odors in the air and neutralizes or modifies those odors.

287
00:15:20,940 --> 00:15:23,700
Here they spray a thousand gallons of the mixture at a time.

288
00:15:24,300 --> 00:15:26,780
So this is air freshener on a giant scale.

289
00:15:27,360 --> 00:15:31,320
Our goal as landfill operators is to make sure that we don't have odors leaving our

290
00:15:31,321 --> 00:15:32,021
site.

291
00:15:32,022 --> 00:15:34,600
100% perfect, but we do our very best to try to knock those

292
00:15:34,700 --> 00:15:36,600
odors down so the neighbors can smell them.

293
00:15:40,600 --> 00:15:43,040
Landfill odor management hasn't always been this

294
00:15:43,140 --> 00:15:47,040
ambitious, but then sanitary landfills didn't exist in this

295
00:15:47,140 --> 00:15:50,560
country until the 1930s.

296
00:15:50,660 --> 00:15:53,960
Before that, dumping trash in the most convenient location

297
00:15:54,060 --> 00:15:59,600
was the norm, oceans, rivers, or an open field.

298
00:15:59,601 --> 00:16:02,940
Smaller cities and towns also used piggeries.

299
00:16:03,040 --> 00:16:06,800
Pig farms set up just to dispose of the town's food waste.

300
00:16:06,900 --> 00:16:11,740
75 pigs could consume about one ton of organic trash a day.

301
00:16:11,840 --> 00:16:14,740
Anything they didn't eat was buried or burnt.

302
00:16:17,860 --> 00:16:19,860
Landfills like Rumpke have come a long way

303
00:16:19,960 --> 00:16:22,960
since the early days of waste management.

304
00:16:23,060 --> 00:16:26,260
And while all this solid waste does create a lot of stink,

305
00:16:26,261 --> 00:16:31,260
What it also generates is natural gas.

306
00:16:31,360 --> 00:16:35,260
Landfill gas is primarily methane and carbon dioxide,

307
00:16:35,360 --> 00:16:38,000
ironically neither of which has odor.

308
00:16:38,100 --> 00:16:40,200
Both gases can be captured and converted

309
00:16:40,300 --> 00:16:43,920
into usable energy, which Rumpke does

310
00:16:44,020 --> 00:16:46,620
at their Kentucky landfill in a process

311
00:16:46,720 --> 00:16:48,920
which begins on the other side of the hill,

312
00:16:49,020 --> 00:16:51,820
where trash trucks are dumping loads.

313
00:16:51,920 --> 00:16:54,720
We're now on the side of the old section of the landfill.

314
00:16:54,721 --> 00:16:57,220
We're standing on about 50 feet of trash,

315
00:16:57,320 --> 00:16:59,660
and this is waste that was placed about 10 years ago.

316
00:16:59,760 --> 00:17:00,960
So it's been placed.

317
00:17:01,060 --> 00:17:02,360
It's been brought up to final grade,

318
00:17:02,460 --> 00:17:04,360
and it's now been covered with soil and seeded.

319
00:17:04,460 --> 00:17:05,760
So this is all vegetated.

320
00:17:05,860 --> 00:17:08,460
And we're standing next to one of our 40 gas wells.

321
00:17:08,560 --> 00:17:11,940
This pipe goes down 100 feet into the landfill waste

322
00:17:12,040 --> 00:17:14,680
below ground, extracting its gases.

323
00:17:14,780 --> 00:17:17,480
You can hear the sound of the gas coming out of the well,

324
00:17:17,580 --> 00:17:20,680
and that's the actual flow of gas coming through the piping.

325
00:17:20,780 --> 00:17:24,720
From the well, gas is pumped to this energy facility.

326
00:17:24,721 --> 00:17:28,960
down into one of these 1,100-horsepower engines.

327
00:17:29,060 --> 00:17:32,720
Each engine burns about 300 cubic feet of gas a minute,

328
00:17:32,820 --> 00:17:37,760
generating a plant total of 3.2 megawatts of electricity.

329
00:17:37,860 --> 00:17:42,080
That's enough to power 2,000 homes in Pendleton County.

330
00:17:42,180 --> 00:17:43,600
Waste that comes into our landfill,

331
00:17:43,700 --> 00:17:46,680
its stinky waste, goes out as odorless energy.

332
00:17:46,780 --> 00:17:49,180
Without a landfill gas collection system at this landfill,

333
00:17:49,280 --> 00:17:52,280
all the landfill gas would just be released into the atmosphere.

334
00:17:52,380 --> 00:17:54,120
And since we're capturing that gas

335
00:17:54,121 --> 00:17:56,760
incinerating it, it's the equivalent of taking 30,000

336
00:17:56,860 --> 00:17:58,760
cars off the street.

337
00:17:58,860 --> 00:18:01,360
The transformation of waste to energy at work here

338
00:18:01,460 --> 00:18:04,460
is the silver lining to a dirty, stinky reality.

339
00:18:06,960 --> 00:18:11,580
The average American generates 4.4 pounds of garbage every day,

340
00:18:11,680 --> 00:18:13,980
or close to one ton annually.

341
00:18:14,080 --> 00:18:18,180
Take the entire country, and that's over 414 billion pounds

342
00:18:18,280 --> 00:18:21,820
of trash a year, the weight equivalent of about 2,500

343
00:18:21,821 --> 00:18:25,060
Nimitz-class aircraft carriers.

344
00:18:25,160 --> 00:18:27,020
Some of the stinkiest garbage we make

345
00:18:27,120 --> 00:18:29,420
probably comes from our kitchens.

346
00:18:29,520 --> 00:18:32,360
The decomposing organic matter sits in our trash cans

347
00:18:32,460 --> 00:18:36,560
for days at a time, releasing odorous gases.

348
00:18:36,660 --> 00:18:38,880
To combat these home intruders, we've

349
00:18:38,980 --> 00:18:43,980
got an arsenal of weapons, like antibacterial sprays,

350
00:18:44,080 --> 00:18:47,920
air fresheners, and now garbage bags that tout anti-stink

351
00:18:48,020 --> 00:18:48,720
capabilities.

352
00:18:51,820 --> 00:18:55,260
But up against this powerful stench of decomposition,

353
00:18:55,360 --> 00:18:57,360
how effective can any garbage bag be?

354
00:19:01,020 --> 00:19:04,900
In the kitchen lab of St. Croix Sensory in Stillwater, Minnesota,

355
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:08,640
scientist Mike McGinley is putting three brands to the test.

356
00:19:08,740 --> 00:19:10,800
So looking at, like, the differences in the bag,

357
00:19:10,900 --> 00:19:13,640
a generic product, thinner material,

358
00:19:13,740 --> 00:19:16,180
versus a name brand product, you know,

359
00:19:16,280 --> 00:19:20,880
a little thicker material, then this is the name brand.

360
00:19:20,881 --> 00:19:22,920
with an odor control technology.

361
00:19:23,020 --> 00:19:25,220
Which, in this case, is a deodorant

362
00:19:25,320 --> 00:19:26,680
that claims to mask the stink.

363
00:19:29,380 --> 00:19:32,520
To test garbage bags, you got to have garbage.

364
00:19:32,620 --> 00:19:35,620
So Mike's whipping up a batch of it.

365
00:19:35,720 --> 00:19:37,940
The St. Croix stink garbage recipe

366
00:19:38,040 --> 00:19:41,940
we've used in some testing of other garbage cans,

367
00:19:42,040 --> 00:19:45,580
different air freshener technologies to put inside a can.

368
00:19:45,680 --> 00:19:48,940
A good garbage recipe is heavy on veggies.

369
00:19:48,941 --> 00:19:51,340
Cabbage itself can be offensive.

370
00:19:51,440 --> 00:19:54,080
A lot of moisture, a lot of sulfur compounds.

371
00:19:54,180 --> 00:19:57,280
Orange peels add some of the acidity to it.

372
00:19:57,380 --> 00:19:59,280
Potato peels will put off a nice stink

373
00:19:59,380 --> 00:20:00,580
as they start to break down.

374
00:20:00,680 --> 00:20:03,180
An apple, the skins are going to really break down

375
00:20:03,280 --> 00:20:05,480
and add to the smell.

376
00:20:05,580 --> 00:20:08,300
And for a final kick to his manufactured stink,

377
00:20:08,400 --> 00:20:12,600
Mike mixes in his secret ingredient, sardines.

378
00:20:12,700 --> 00:20:15,000
It adds good protein to the mix.

379
00:20:15,100 --> 00:20:17,440
It's going to give it some fishy smell,

380
00:20:17,441 --> 00:20:21,540
but really gives the stew some things to get the bugs growing,

381
00:20:21,820 --> 00:20:23,200
really get the mixture cooking.

382
00:20:26,440 --> 00:20:30,340
Mike puts the mixture into a garbage bag to stew for 48 hours

383
00:20:30,341 --> 00:20:33,600
and let the rotting food's stench reach maximum potential.

384
00:20:35,460 --> 00:20:37,040
Even just a few minutes in,

385
00:20:37,400 --> 00:20:39,600
the smell is already registering on the stink scale.

386
00:20:41,080 --> 00:20:42,760
Right now on the nasal ranger,

387
00:20:42,880 --> 00:20:45,760
this is probably going to be around a seven.

388
00:20:45,761 --> 00:20:49,760
There's a decent smell here, but it's not all that offensive.

389
00:20:49,761 --> 00:20:53,760
A couple days, hopefully we're hitting towards the 60.

390
00:20:53,761 --> 00:20:57,760
Two days later, Mike uncovers his garbage-making triumph.

391
00:20:57,761 --> 00:21:00,760
Cabbage, potato peels.

392
00:21:00,761 --> 00:21:04,760
You can see the slop in there is really the sardines.

393
00:21:04,761 --> 00:21:08,760
I mean, look at just how gooey and slimy it is.

394
00:21:08,761 --> 00:21:11,760
The 48-hour-old trash is now putting off a stink much stronger

395
00:21:11,761 --> 00:21:14,760
than a field of cow manure.

396
00:21:14,761 --> 00:21:17,860
That's enough to nauseate our Modern Marbles crew.

397
00:21:17,960 --> 00:21:19,460
This is disgusting.

398
00:21:21,960 --> 00:21:23,960
Are you kidding me?

399
00:21:24,060 --> 00:21:26,800
With the trash hitting maximum stink,

400
00:21:26,900 --> 00:21:30,900
it's time for Mike and his team to test the garbage bags.

401
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,320
Under each of the silver domes is a different brand

402
00:21:33,420 --> 00:21:35,620
of bag containing some of Mike's garbage

403
00:21:35,720 --> 00:21:39,520
that trained odor assessors are smelling today.

404
00:21:39,620 --> 00:21:41,920
You know, stink, some of it's contextual.

405
00:21:41,921 --> 00:21:49,320
You know, it's essentially stink comes down to what's an offensive odor to people at the time they're experiencing it.

406
00:21:49,600 --> 00:21:57,100
Basically, what we found in the garbage bag testing that we did was that the two brand name products were definitely better than the generic brand.

407
00:21:57,240 --> 00:21:58,420
Pretty much what we expected.

408
00:21:58,660 --> 00:22:05,460
But between the two brand name products, one with the odor control technology and one without, there was only a slight difference.

409
00:22:05,461 --> 00:22:10,480
Where the one with the odor control technology did do slightly better, but not a really large difference.

410
00:22:10,481 --> 00:22:12,480
But fancy trash bags or not,

411
00:22:12,481 --> 00:22:15,480
the battle against odor rages on.

412
00:22:15,481 --> 00:22:18,480
And that's good news from Mike and his colleagues.

413
00:22:18,481 --> 00:22:20,480
St. Croix Sensory's been around for 30 years.

414
00:22:20,481 --> 00:22:23,480
Through it all, everything stinks,

415
00:22:23,481 --> 00:22:26,480
so we know there'll always be business for us.

416
00:22:34,480 --> 00:22:37,480
On our smelly journey, we've encountered a variety of stinks

417
00:22:37,481 --> 00:22:43,120
variety of stinks made by man, animals, and vegetables.

418
00:22:43,220 --> 00:22:45,520
But then there are those that come directly from the Earth.

419
00:22:48,820 --> 00:22:51,620
Yellowstone National Park is renowned for its pristine

420
00:22:51,720 --> 00:22:54,640
natural beauty.

421
00:22:54,740 --> 00:22:57,740
But the park is also famous for its powerful stink.

422
00:22:57,840 --> 00:22:59,640
It just reeks out here.

423
00:22:59,740 --> 00:23:01,940
From its hundreds of geysers and other

424
00:23:02,040 --> 00:23:05,840
hydrothermal features.

425
00:23:05,841 --> 00:23:09,840
The stinkiest of all is mud volcano.

426
00:23:09,940 --> 00:23:13,640
This giant crater of hot mud churns at 184 degrees

427
00:23:13,740 --> 00:23:15,780
Fahrenheit.

428
00:23:15,880 --> 00:23:19,080
As heat rises from deep in the earth and boils the mud,

429
00:23:19,180 --> 00:23:20,980
it breaks up the surrounding rock

430
00:23:21,080 --> 00:23:24,200
that contains high levels of sulfur.

431
00:23:24,300 --> 00:23:26,960
That releases hydrogen sulfide gas

432
00:23:27,060 --> 00:23:30,340
that comes with a characteristically unpleasant smell.

433
00:23:30,440 --> 00:23:31,740
Rotten eggs.

434
00:23:31,840 --> 00:23:33,440
It's got an eggy smell.

435
00:23:33,441 --> 00:23:35,440
It's like...

436
00:23:35,441 --> 00:23:36,440
axe.

437
00:23:36,441 --> 00:23:38,440
A dirty axe.

438
00:23:38,441 --> 00:23:41,440
Hydrogen sulfide smells awful to us

439
00:23:41,441 --> 00:23:45,440
because we are extremely sensitive to this gas.

440
00:23:45,441 --> 00:23:47,440
I can't imagine we smell anything worse.

441
00:23:47,441 --> 00:23:49,440
Broken toilet?

442
00:23:49,441 --> 00:23:52,440
Normally, humans in the animal world

443
00:23:52,441 --> 00:23:55,440
have a very minimal sense of smell,

444
00:23:55,441 --> 00:23:59,440
but we can detect very, very small amounts of that gas.

445
00:23:59,441 --> 00:24:02,440
That's a good thing, because, you see,

446
00:24:02,441 --> 00:24:06,440
is a poison in higher enough concentration.

447
00:24:08,440 --> 00:24:11,440
The human sense of smell really was optimized

448
00:24:11,441 --> 00:24:13,440
to be a warning system.

449
00:24:13,441 --> 00:24:15,440
It really tells us first and foremost

450
00:24:15,441 --> 00:24:17,440
when there's something in our environment

451
00:24:17,441 --> 00:24:20,440
that we should pay attention to and perhaps leave,

452
00:24:20,441 --> 00:24:22,440
because it might be harmful.

453
00:24:22,441 --> 00:24:25,440
As it turns out, the human nose is far more complicated

454
00:24:25,441 --> 00:24:28,440
than we really appreciate or give it credit for.

455
00:24:28,441 --> 00:24:31,440
Our sense of smell begins when odor molecules

456
00:24:31,441 --> 00:24:34,680
when odor molecules from our environment enter our nose,

457
00:24:34,780 --> 00:24:37,840
where they trigger odor receptors.

458
00:24:37,940 --> 00:24:41,420
Different odor molecules activate different receptors.

459
00:24:41,520 --> 00:24:44,420
The pattern of that activation

460
00:24:44,520 --> 00:24:47,160
is what gets sent to the brain, and that tells us

461
00:24:47,260 --> 00:24:50,420
whether we're smelling the odor from diesel exhaust

462
00:24:50,520 --> 00:24:53,300
or something good like a slice of pizza.

463
00:24:53,400 --> 00:24:56,260
Or something really bad, like one of our country's

464
00:24:56,360 --> 00:25:00,100
most problematic livestock smells.

465
00:25:00,101 --> 00:25:03,540
U.S. hog farming is a $19 billion industry

466
00:25:03,541 --> 00:25:07,260
that produces more than 66 million pigs every year

467
00:25:07,261 --> 00:25:11,380
and almost 400,000 tons of manure every day.

468
00:25:12,140 --> 00:25:14,180
Much is stored in swine lagoons,

469
00:25:14,500 --> 00:25:16,600
open pits of waste 20 feet deep

470
00:25:16,601 --> 00:25:19,940
and measuring up to four times the size of a football field.

471
00:25:20,800 --> 00:25:23,540
The airborne stench of a swine lagoon can be overwhelming,

472
00:25:23,820 --> 00:25:26,700
and it's a growing problem in big hog farm states

473
00:25:26,701 --> 00:25:28,580
like North Carolina and Iowa.

474
00:25:30,100 --> 00:25:38,500
Meet Fastlane, a 600-pound adult male hog who lives in central Iowa.

475
00:25:39,500 --> 00:25:45,000
Fastlane and his fellow pigs are part of a very important stink project at this research farm at

476
00:25:45,001 --> 00:25:52,931
Iowa State University. In Iowa, we have about 15 million pigs at any given time.

477
00:25:52,932 --> 00:25:54,220
There is only

478
00:25:54,221 --> 00:25:55,920
about 3 million people.

479
00:25:56,860 --> 00:25:59,000
That's where typically we would have

480
00:25:59,001 --> 00:26:01,520
older complaint problems.

481
00:26:02,220 --> 00:26:05,280
Dr. Yasek Koziel is an expert in pig manure,

482
00:26:05,740 --> 00:26:08,380
its powerful stench, and how to reduce it.

483
00:26:09,480 --> 00:26:14,000
Yes, definitely my job stinks, but I love it.

484
00:26:15,380 --> 00:26:17,740
Your average adult pig will produce

485
00:26:17,741 --> 00:26:19,940
about 12 pounds of manure every day.

486
00:26:20,740 --> 00:26:23,620
So Dr. Koziel can collect plenty of samples

487
00:26:23,621 --> 00:26:25,620
from the farm's outside storage pit.

488
00:26:28,920 --> 00:26:30,220
And going in.

489
00:26:31,720 --> 00:26:34,060
There is nice crust.

490
00:26:34,061 --> 00:26:37,360
My kids know that daddy works with the poop,

491
00:26:37,361 --> 00:26:41,840
and that's really, that really starts a great conversation,

492
00:26:41,841 --> 00:26:43,540
if you can imagine.

493
00:26:43,541 --> 00:26:46,240
Okay, so here it comes.

494
00:26:46,241 --> 00:26:49,340
Nice manure.

495
00:26:49,341 --> 00:26:51,440
Sample.

496
00:26:51,441 --> 00:26:52,720
There we go.

497
00:26:53,620 --> 00:26:57,620
Dr. Koziel will take this prize back to his lab

498
00:26:57,621 --> 00:27:00,620
to unlock the secrets of its stink.

499
00:27:00,621 --> 00:27:04,620
I'm sure it smells pretty interesting.

500
00:27:04,621 --> 00:27:07,620
Dr. Koziel's enthusiasm about pig manure

501
00:27:07,621 --> 00:27:10,620
demonstrates the subjective nature of stink.

502
00:27:10,621 --> 00:27:14,620
For someone who's around the smell of swine manure all the time,

503
00:27:14,621 --> 00:27:17,620
it can be a daily delight.

504
00:27:17,621 --> 00:27:20,620
But to others, like our Modern Marvels crew,

505
00:27:20,621 --> 00:27:23,620
the high ammonia stink can be unbearable.

506
00:27:23,621 --> 00:27:24,620
This is pretty bad.

507
00:27:24,621 --> 00:27:26,620
It smells like...

508
00:27:26,621 --> 00:27:27,620
You can say it.

509
00:27:27,621 --> 00:27:29,620
It smells like a bag of cakes.

510
00:27:29,621 --> 00:27:30,620
Seriously, I don't want to be on this.

511
