or testing purposes.

616
00:32:35,560 --> 00:32:39,320
It's just like perfume, except that it's essence of cow manure

617
00:32:39,321 --> 00:32:41,460
or garbage or bathroom stench.

618
00:32:43,420 --> 00:32:47,420
But some manufactured stinks play an even more essential role

619
00:32:47,421 --> 00:32:50,620
in our day-to-day lives, like the rotten egg smell

620
00:32:50,621 --> 00:32:52,520
that's added to odorless natural gas,

621
00:32:52,720 --> 00:32:55,900
so the potentially dangerous leaks can be detected.

622
00:32:57,040 --> 00:32:59,900
The artificial stink we add to natural gas

623
00:32:59,901 --> 00:33:03,040
is mainly the result of one of the worst school tragedies

624
00:33:03,041 --> 00:33:03,940
in U.S. history.

625
00:33:04,680 --> 00:33:07,380
It is with deep regret that we bring you

626
00:33:07,381 --> 00:33:09,360
these graphic appalling scenes

627
00:33:09,361 --> 00:33:12,120
of one of the greatest tragedies in American history.

628
00:33:12,440 --> 00:33:16,020
On March 18, 1937, in New London, Texas,

629
00:33:16,340 --> 00:33:18,940
a natural gas leak in the local school

630
00:33:18,941 --> 00:33:20,780
fueled a massive explosion,

631
00:33:21,480 --> 00:33:23,200
killing hundreds of students and teachers

632
00:33:23,201 --> 00:33:25,180
and injuring countless others.

633
00:33:25,800 --> 00:33:26,500
The cause?

634
00:33:26,600 --> 00:33:29,660
A faulty pipe through which natural gas had been leaking.

635
00:33:29,900 --> 00:33:32,540
undetected for days.

636
00:33:32,640 --> 00:33:35,540
In the somber aftermath, the Texas state legislature

637
00:33:35,640 --> 00:33:39,640
mandated that natural gas be injected with odorous chemicals,

638
00:33:39,740 --> 00:33:42,640
called mercaptans, to help detect leaks

639
00:33:42,740 --> 00:33:44,140
and prevent future disasters.

640
00:33:46,720 --> 00:33:51,660
Today, all gas refineries have to add mercaptans to natural gas,

641
00:33:51,760 --> 00:33:55,900
which is why a gas leak smells like rotten eggs.

642
00:33:56,000 --> 00:33:59,900
Stink and all, this is a smell that saves lives.

643
00:33:59,901 --> 00:34:07,340
Now, most of the natural gas we consume is extracted from fossil fuels deep underground.

644
00:34:07,341 --> 00:34:11,760
But in the United States, there's another major source of natural gas that goes mostly

645
00:34:11,761 --> 00:34:14,260
unused.

646
00:34:14,261 --> 00:34:19,160
Cows are notoriously gassy, and there's no doubt the 100 million of them in the U.S.

647
00:34:19,161 --> 00:34:25,660
alone make for noxious amounts of greenhouse gases let loose into the atmosphere.

648
00:34:25,661 --> 00:34:31,000
Some studies claim cows create even more pollution than cars.

649
00:34:31,001 --> 00:34:36,480
The reason behind such flatulence is a highly complex digestive system.

650
00:34:36,481 --> 00:34:42,040
Cows digest through a four-part stomach, repeatedly chewing and regurgitating their food.

651
00:34:42,041 --> 00:34:48,040
A cow's first digestive compartment, called the rumen, is where bacteria start processing

652
00:34:48,041 --> 00:34:50,900
food, producing large amounts of gas.

653
00:34:50,901 --> 00:34:55,280
Those gases will eventually build up and it creates pressure, and that's when they

654
00:34:55,281 --> 00:34:58,180
they actually what we call eructate, which is essentially

655
00:34:58,181 --> 00:35:01,880
belching out those excess gases because of that high pressure

656
00:35:01,881 --> 00:35:04,320
that built up in the rumen.

657
00:35:04,321 --> 00:35:07,400
At Iowa State University, Dr. Stephanie Hanson

658
00:35:07,401 --> 00:35:09,660
is working on reducing cow emissions

659
00:35:09,661 --> 00:35:13,740
by changing up their diet.

660
00:35:13,741 --> 00:35:17,100
In the US, many cows eat a processed corn-based feed

661
00:35:17,101 --> 00:35:19,840
that contains sulfuric acid.

662
00:35:19,841 --> 00:35:23,180
What happens is that when that sulfur gets into the rumen,

663
00:35:23,181 --> 00:35:26,620
It actually eventually gets transformed into hydrogen sulfide gas,

664
00:35:26,621 --> 00:35:31,140
so that rotten egg odor smell that you might be familiar with, that's hydrogen sulfide.

665
00:35:31,980 --> 00:35:34,520
At the university's beef nutrition farm,

666
00:35:35,160 --> 00:35:38,520
Dr. Hansen and her team are experimenting with different cow diets,

667
00:35:39,000 --> 00:35:40,520
engaging the gassy results.

668
00:35:41,420 --> 00:35:46,300
Because here, monitoring a cow's digestion is at their fingertips, literally.

669
00:35:46,940 --> 00:35:49,180
This steer has what we call a rumen fistula.

670
00:35:49,181 --> 00:35:52,520
This guy's undergone a surgical modification when he was much younger,

671
00:35:52,521 --> 00:35:56,620
and we've basically put a window to his sole, so to speak,

672
00:35:56,660 --> 00:35:57,980
a window into his rumen here.

673
00:35:58,480 --> 00:36:01,200
This allows us to use these guys as a great research tool

674
00:36:01,201 --> 00:36:03,440
because we can get direct access into the rumen.

675
00:36:04,100 --> 00:36:06,620
These steers don't feel any pain when we're doing our sampling.

676
00:36:06,800 --> 00:36:08,940
They don't have any nerve endings or anything that are being affected

677
00:36:08,941 --> 00:36:11,040
while we're sampling, so the steers don't feel any pain.

678
00:36:12,280 --> 00:36:13,940
Just like in the cow's bellies,

679
00:36:14,200 --> 00:36:16,860
the gas production in the bottles happens fast.

680
00:36:16,861 --> 00:36:22,680
That's the pressure from all the gases being produced.

681
00:36:23,760 --> 00:36:26,060
And you can definitely smell the hydrogen sulfide.

682
00:36:26,720 --> 00:36:31,080
Each of these small samples shows just what prolific gas makers cows can be.

683
00:36:31,580 --> 00:36:35,340
So obviously when you're talking about a 1,200-pound steer, you've got a lot of gases.

684
00:36:35,960 --> 00:36:40,640
Which is why Dr. Hansen's work has an immediate impact on the war against stink.

685
00:36:40,860 --> 00:36:44,360
Decreasing the amount of gases cattle would produce would certainly be a win-win

686
00:36:44,361 --> 00:36:47,980
because not only would it be decreasing potential greenhouse gas effects,

687
00:36:47,981 --> 00:36:51,300
but also decreasing the odors associated perhaps with cattle production.

688
00:36:57,720 --> 00:37:01,200
On this odorous journey exploring the stench of our world,

689
00:37:01,660 --> 00:37:04,980
there's one set of smells we haven't put our noses up against yet.

